    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID54",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q30376f88cb1e79a8-chunk_4e42631439af21409a5d3ea7",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.3 dehumanising attacks and overt sexual objectification</strong> – Derogating women by comparing them to non-human entities such as vermin, disease or refuse, or overtly reducing them to sexual objects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p>2. Online experiences of gender-based harassment<br><br><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">A. Being online                                         “I remember this one specific incident, when\nGirls who face harassment simply for existing           I was young… I used to get a lot of messages\nonline reported harassment which was mainly sexual      from boys asking me to send nudes or\nin nature:                                              blackmailing me about a picture that I posted\n                                                        that they’re going share it or edit it in a bad\n➜ receiving sexual comments or explicit messages,       way and share it with everyone if I don’t do\n  including propositions of transactional sex,          this or that. Or just generally talking, like\n➜ receiving sexual or explicit photos or images,        saying bad words to me. At that young age it\n                                                        was, honestly, horrible. So, it was the worst\n➜ receiving requests to send photos of themselves,      time in my life, using social media. Between\n  including examples of blackmail,                      the age of 9 and 14.”\n➜ unauthorised sharing of content and/or                               Young woman, 20, Sudan\n  demonstrating knowledge of the girl’s personal       Young women in the qualitative interviews also\n  information.\n                                                       described being sent sexual images, graphic photos\n                                                       of genitalia or pornography:\n “…what happened to me it’s when a guy just\n tried to hold something about me on social             “Actually it happens even if I don’t post\n media, and saying, ‘What currency would you            anything… It’s just like, men have this\n like? Would you like dollars? Would you like           mentality where they can just play around\n euro? What do you want? …You know, just                with you, that kind of thing, they start\n one night I can give you a lot of money that           sending you explicit photos of themselves…\n will change your life. I can give you all the          having a guy just send me a naked photo\n money in this world. Come and fuck you and             of himself… is really freaky.…They just go\n everything.’ Can you imagine such things?              into your inbox: ‘Hey babe,’ and then I open\n Yeah. I felt very bad.”                                this message and I freak out, ’What the hell\n  Young woman, 23, South Sudan                          is happening?’… They are sending you\n                                                        things without your consent, which is just\n                                                        disrespectful.”\n                                                        Young woman, 23, Malawi\nSexual objectification is very much part of a girl’s social media experience, with the way girls are dressed or present themselves online being subject to intense and critical scrutiny.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q30376f88cb1e79a8-chunk_4e42631439af21409a5d3ea7",
